the man who fell to earth it's got naked  David Bowie  what else could you ask for  I'll be sticking with my usual short  summary instead of a full synopsis of  the film but if you want a more detailed  rundown of the story and plot then I  highly recommend brows held highs  musical review it's both incredibly  inventive and deeply entertaining and  was in part what spurred me to give my  own take on the film in summary the film  is about Thomas Jerome Newton who  stumbles out of the hills one day sells  some gold for cash hires a lawyer gets  some patents gets rich moves his company  to New Mexico meets a hotel clerk -  named Mary Lou and she introduces him to  sex television and booze he gets his  company to build a rocket reveals to  Mary Lou that he's an alien and gets  double-crossed by one of his employees  named Nathan Bryce Newton is detained by  the government held captive and drunk in  a luxury suite tested and tortured as  gun sex with Mary Lou is released from  captivity than meets Bryce at a cafe  where they share drunken profundities  and the film ends now what first  inspired me to do this episode was  actually the gun sex scene towards the  end there in the Browse held high  episode this bizarre scene was hamed up  for its joke potential because well it  is pretty insane  but that said it's also the lynchpin on  one of the running metaphors in the film  which is the relationship between  audience industry and artists I've  talked about this issue before in the  news head episode but here we have a  completely different take on the matter  the running metaphor compares creators  whether they be inventors or artists to  aliens in the film Newton is literally  an alien and is our metaphoric prototype  for an artist he invents fantastic  technology because he comes from a more  advanced civilization on another planet  and the government keeps an eye on him  because they're worried that his  company's giant leaps in technology will  destabilize the economy and social  balance problem with this corporation is  that it is technologically  overstimulated and the economic trouble  stems from that fact in the real world  artisan inventors drive society forward  with new ideas and technologies both of  which governments throughout the ages  have been quick to suppress or control  in the real world artisan inventors are  typically viewed as being something  other than normal people who write  stories must lead kind of interesting  lives too  I know I love you I can character in  this story not so much for the raw  mechanical talent required to make  something but rather for their unique  outlook on life in the world one of the  most common questions asked of any  artist is where do you get your ideas  from it's a question that belies a  deeper view of creativity and ideas is  something alien to normal human  operation like artists are headed out  into the hills with burlap sacks that  they bring home brimming with freshly  harvested ideas in reality creativity  stems from a different way of taking in  the stimuli of everyday life be it a  sensitivity to music or an awareness of  culture and passion in the film Newton  literally experiences a different  reality he can see x-rays and hears  things from a great distance away and  this brings us to the infamous scene gun  sex I said earlier that this is a  linchpin scene so let's see if we can  fold out why to do this I'm going to  describe the plot again but this time  I'm going to do it in terms of metaphor  the artist comes out into the world from  the background of conflict hearing and  seeing things that the rest of the world  is oblivious to he uses his history and  insight to produce products that attract  the attention of the established culture  and power structure let's call them his  label in his fame and fortune he gets  distracted from his goal by sex media  and substance abuse he tries to break  out of the cycle but is restrained by  his label who locked him in a gilded  cage and continued to coldly exploit him  for their own game to the point that  they burn his unique worldview out of  him he is left desperate but creatively  emptied to the point that even his worst  ideas his most passionate and  destructive ideas are impotent  he's now shooting blanks and having been  used up the label casually discards him  to an ignominious fate in the film the  gun sex scene is the lowest point Newton  is creatively spent in powerless to his  captors and his addictions  and that's what I see in the man who  fell to earth it's not a perfect film by  any means the editing is odd in places  even by mid 70 standards and the pacing  is slow but it's still well worth  watching if only because nowadays it'll  be a cold day in hell before Hollywood  make something like this instead of  transformers 5 return of the cash money  robots also in some ways the film's  flaws work in its favor there are  extended metaphors and themes but these  elements aren't nearly as pervasive as  they might be in a more structurally  sound film which in these kind of movies  typically leads to symbolism overload  the surrealist sides and non sequiturs  often don't make sense and while it may  not be intentional the result is a world  that's more complicated and haphazard  than most narrative worlds more chaotic  and more like the real world the  characters aren't always consistent but  that's their consistency they make  decisions based on flawed or poorly  thought-out rationale just like real  people do it's not amazing storytelling  but it is a great story next week  brace yourself we're going to look at  the worst kind of storytelling  unintelligible pretentious crap  never know  it's their life on Mars
 

